"Agent Trust Index - Vouch Protocol - Vouch Protocol (https://vouch-protocol.com/agent-trust-index/)\nciteturn97view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://vouch-protocol.com/agent-trust-index/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 627\nL0: 【0†Vouch Protocol】\nL1: \nL2: For the ecosystem\nL3: # The Agent Trust Index\nL4: \nL5: An open benchmark of how trustworthy the agent world actually is. It checks one simple thing for each public agent: can it cryptographically prove who it is, on whose authority it acts, and that it has not been tampered with?\nL6: \nL7: The first sweep checked 60,451 unique agents from the public Model Context Protocol registry on 9 November 2026. The answer is uncomfortable.\nL8: \nL9: 99.2%\nL10: \nL11: of the 60,451 agents checked cannot present an identity anyone can verify.\nL12: \nL13: 0.8%\nL14: can prove who they are with a resolvable did:web identity. That is 495 agents.\nL15: \nL16: 4\nL17: \nL18: agents are post-quantum ready. Not one carries an ML-DSA key.\nL19: \nL20: As of 9 November 2026. First sweep of 60,451 agents from the public Model Context Protocol registry.\nL21: \nL22: Share this\nL23: \nL24: Is your agent one of them?\nL25: \nL26: Run the same check the Index runs, on your own domain. You get a letter grade, the exact reasons, and the fixes to raise it, in about ten seconds.\nL27: \nL28: 【1†Grade your agent →】\nL29: ## Most agents are anonymous. Yours does not have to be.\nL30: \nL31: Give your agent a verifiable identity in one command. It is free and open source, and you keep control of your own keys.\nL32: \nL33:     `pip install vouch-protocol`\nL34: \nL35: 【2†Get started】【3†View on GitHub†github.com】\nL36: ## Identity is the floor. Accountability is the core.\nL37: \nL38: Knowing which agent acted is only the start. The harder questions are who authorized it, whether it is still behaving, and whether it can be stopped. A bare identity does not answer those. The Vouch Protocol does, and this Index will grow to measure all of it.\nL39: \nL40: ### Who authorized it\nL41: \nL42: A delegation chain shows who gave the agent its authority, on whose behalf, and within what limits, all verifiable.\nL43: ### Is it still trustworthy\nL44: \nL45: Continuous trust means an agent has to keep proving itself, not get trusted once and forever.\nL46: \nL47: ### Can it be stopped\nL48: \nL49: Revocation lets you pull an agent's authority the moment it goes wrong, and anyone can check the status.\nL50: ## What the first sweep found\nL51: \nL52: As of 9 November 2026\nL53: \nL54: Trust property  | Agents  | Share\nL55: --- | --- | ---\nL56: Can prove who they are (resolvable did:web identity)  | 495  | 0.8%\nL57: Agent card references a key or signature  | 921  | 1.5%\nL58: Publishes a service endpoint (revocation, MCP, A2A)  | 257  | 0.4%\nL59: Post-quantum ready (an ML-DSA key)  | 4  | 0%\nL60: Two properties a static scan cannot see are left out on purpose: delegation provenance (who authorized the agent) and continuous trust (renewed, not trusted once). Both are runtime properties.\nL61: ## The agents that can prove it\nL62: \nL63: The 495 agents that publish a verifiable signal, scored from 0 to 100 and graded, as of 9 November 2026. The other 59,956 scored zero. Filter by grade.\nL64: \nL65: All (495)A (201)B (0)C (294)D (0)\nL66: Grade  | Score  | Agent  | Domain  | Method\nL67: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL68: A  | 100  | ai.achromat/mcp-server  | mom.achromat.ai  | did:web, Ed25519 (Multikey)\nL69: A  | 100  | ai.apir/verify  | apir.ai  | did:web, Ed25519 (JWK)\nL70: A  | 100  | ai.councilof/gspc  | councilof.ai  | did:web, Ed25519 (JWK)\nL71: A  | 100  | ai.councilof/gspc-free  | councilof.ai  | did:web, Ed25519 (JWK)\nL72: A  | 100  | ai.inflowpay.app/inflow  | app.inflowpay.ai  | did:web, P-256 (JWK)\nL73: A  | 100  | ai.pubfi/mcp  | mcp.pubfi.ai  | did:web, Ed25519 (JWK)\nL74: A  | 100  | ai.snowdata/live-snow  | mcp.snowdata.ai  