edoracle/stablecoin-risk  | feedoracle.io  | did:web, secp256k1 (JWK)\nL162: A  | 100  | io.github.freeq-irc/freeq  | irc.freeq.at  | did:web, Ed25519 (Multikey)\nL163: A  | 100  | io.github.huwhitememes/tollbooth  | agenttoll.dev  | did:web, P-256 (JWK)\nL164: A  | 100  | io.github.inamprotocol/inam-mcp  | api.inamprotocol.org  | did:key, key\nL165: A  | 100  | io.github.indexagent/scrypta-mcp  | scrypta.com.br  | did:web, Ed25519 (JWK)\nL166: A  | 100  | io.github.jai-rathore/agentboard-skills  | agentboard-api.onrender.com  | did:key, Ed25519\nL167: A  | 100  | io.github.jake-zaiba/deai-research  | research.zaiba.cloud  | did:web, Ed25519 (Multikey)\nL168: A  | 100  | io.github.jweezy119/Pabandi  | pabandi.onrender.com  | did:web, P-256 (JWK)\nL169: A  | 100  | io.github.lbailey94/whitemagic-mcp  | mcp.whitemagic.dev  | did:key, key\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nModel Context Problems (https://modelcontextproblem.com/)\nciteturn97view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://modelcontextproblem.com/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3576\nL0: 【0†timeline】 【1†about】 【2†rss】\nL1: # 【0†model context problems】\nL2: \nL3: a running record of publicly disclosed security incidents in the Model Context Protocol ecosystem. because what could possibly go wrong when you let language models autonomously talk to everything?\nL4: \nL5: 181\nL6: \nL7: incidents tracked\nL8: \nL9: 46\nL10: \nL11: critical severity\nL12: \nL13: 83\nL14: \nL15: high severity\nL16: \nL17: 268\nL18: \nL19: CVEs assigned\nL20: \nL21: numbers update as new incidents are disclosed.\nL22: filter: [Button: all] [Button: critical ][Button: high ][Button: medium ][Button: low ][Button: informational ] [Button: cve assigned] [Button: #advisory ][Button: #authorization ][Button: #authentication ][Button: #rce ][Button: #credential-theft ][Button: #data-leak ][Button: #ssrf ][Button: #protocol-design ]\nL23: \nL24: 2026 · november · 05\nL25: \nL26: [medium] no CVE assigned\nL27: ## The Official Rust SDK's Client Reads Whatever the Server Sends, All of It 【3†#】\nL28: DaleSeo published GHSA-c9f9-cp8h-jcj5 on November 5, 2026 against `rmcp` 0.2.0 before 3.9.0, rated Moderate at 6.5 under CVSS 3.1 with no CVE, crediting kalman5 and, as an independent reporter of the same finding, jlockwood10. The built-in Streamable HTTP clients, the reqwest one and the Unix socket one, read non-streaming response bodies into memory with no size limit, on successful `application/json` responses and on non-2xx error responses alike.\nL29: A server answering with endless whitespace, or with a 500 that never ends, grows the client's memory until the host application dies, and it works during `initialize`, before authentication. The SSE path is unaffected, because each event is bounded by `max_sse_event_size`, 16 MiB by default, and events are handled one at a time. Version 3.9.0 caps successful JSON bodies at 16 MiB and non-success bodies at 64 KiB, raising `StreamableHttpError::ResponseBodyTooLarge` rather than truncating.\nL30: commentary\nL31: \nL32: The SSE transport has carried a configurable cap the whole time. The JSON path next to it had none, which is the familiar shape of a limit added only where somebody happened to be thinking about limits. Note the direction of travel: the server is the attacker here and the client is the target, which is backwards from most of this site.\nL33: \nL34: impact\nL35: \nL36: A malicious server kills any Rust MCP client that connects to it, including during the handshake, by answering with a body that does not end.\nL37: \nL38: sources\nL39: 【4†GHSA-c9f9-cp8h-jcj5: Unbounded response-body buffering in rmcp Streamable HTTP clients allows memory exhaustion†github.com】\nL40: \nL41: tags [Button: denial-of-service][Button: protocol-design][Button: advisory]\nL42: \nL43: 2026 · november · 03\nL44: \nL45: [medium] CVE-2026-122140\nL46: ## Trawl Checks for a Loopback Spelling Its Own URL Parser Never Produces 【5†#】\nL47: germondai published GHSA-mj45-84p2-v77q on November 3, 2026 against Trawl 1.5.0 through 1.9.3, crediting Leardian, rated Moderate at 5.3 under CVSS 3.1. The outbound policy in `apps/api/src/outbound-policy.ts` recognised IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses only in their dotted-decimal form, so `::ffff:127.0.0.1` was refused while the hexadecimal `::ffff:7f00:1` fell through to the default not-private answer.\nL48: URL parsing normalises the dotted form into hexadecimal before the check runs, so every spelling was affected in practice. MCP is off by default behind `MCP_ENABLED`, and when it is on the validator runs on the scrape handler, before each HTTP hop and on manual redirects. Version 1.10.0 replaces the regular expression checks with numeric subnet classification and enforces public egress at connection time.\nL49: The reporter confirmed a mapped-address request reaching a loopback listener both directly and through a redirect from an allowed page.\nL50: 【6†Image: anime guy gesturing at a butterfly: is this a private address?】 EX.A anime guy gesturing at a butterfly: is this a private address?\nL51: \nL52: commentary\nL53: \nL54: The filter knew `::ffff:127.0.0.1` and the layer above it handed over `::ffff:7f00:1`. Writing an address check against the spelling a human types, rather than the one your own parser emits, is the entire bug.\nL55: \nL56: impact\nL57: On an instance with MCP enabled, anyone who can supply a scrape URL, or get an allowed page to redirect, pulls responses from loopback and private-range destinations.\nL58: \nL59: sources\nL60: \nL61: 【7†GHSA-mj45-84p2-v77q: MCP public-destination bypass via IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses†github.com】【8†NVD record for CVE-2026-122140, the identifier VulnCheck assigned†nvd.nist.gov】\nL62: \nL63: tags [Button: ssrf][Button: local-network][Button: advisory]\nL64: \nL65: 2026 · november · 03\nL66: \nL67: [medium] CVE-2026-121458\nL68: ## Instatic's MCP Read Tools Skip the Check Its HTTP API Runs 【9†#】\nL69: DavidBabinec published GHSA-x5cc-8486-8qqx on November 3, 2026, crediting Sriman Aluvala, rated Moderate at 6.5 under CVSS 3.1. The `content_get_document`, `content_list_documents` and `content_search_documents` tools in `server/ai/tools/content/readTools.ts` check only that the caller holds a required capability, never whether that caller may read a specific table or a specific row.\nL70: A user holding `ai.chat` and `content.edit.own` reads other users' unpublished drafts, enumerates rows including their `authorUserId` values, and reaches system-table rows without the capability for them. The same request against the ordinary HTTP data-row endpoint returns 404. The reporter reproduced it on 0.0.20 and traced the behaviour back to 0.0.5; 0.0.21 fixes it. VulnCheck filed CVE-2026-121458 the same day and scores it 7.1 under CVSS 4.0.\nL71: commentary\nL72: \nL73: The 404 is the tell. The authorization boundary exists and is correct, and it lives in the HTTP handler, so tools that reach the data layer directly inherit exactly none of it.\nL74: \nL75: impact\nL76: \nL77: Any low-privileged account with chat and own-content capabilities reads every other user's private drafts through the MCP surface.\nL78: \nL79: sources\nL80: 【10†GHSA-x5cc-8486-8qqx: Missing authorization in Instatic AI/MCP content read tools†github.com】【11†VulnCheck: Instatic authorization bypass via MCP content read tools (CVE-2026-121458)†www.vulncheck.com】\nL81: \nL82: tags [Button: authorization][Button: data-leak][Button: advisory]\nL83: \nL84: 2026 · november · 02\nL85: \nL86: [medium] CVE-2026-116635\nL87: ## AWS Ships Its Third MCP Bulletin in Eleven Days 【12†#】\nL88: AWS published security bulletin 2026-164 on November 2, 2026, covering CVE-2026-116635 in `awslabs.aws-serverless-mcp-server` before 0.2.1 and crediting Syed Anas Mohiuddin through coordinated disclosure. The deployment status component takes a project name supplied through the MCP agent and joins it into a path without confining the result, so directory traversal sequences or an absolute path reach JSON files outside the intended deployment status directory.\nL89: Where write access has been enabled, the same input creates or overwrites JSON files outside it. AWS says no workarounds are available and tells operators to patch forked and derivative code too. The matching advisory, GHSA-r6mm-377j-qg8m, went up on `awslabs/mcp` the same day.\nL90: 【13†Image: charlie at the string board: three aws mcp bulletins in eleven days】 EX.A charlie at the string board: three aws mcp bulletins in eleven days\nL91: \nL92: commentary\nL93: \nL94: Bulletin 2026-149 on October 23, 2026-152 on October 27, this one on November 2. Three different AWS MCP servers, one recurring assumption: that the thing on the other end of a tool call is a configuration value rather than attacker input.\nL95: \nL96: impact\nL97: An attacker who can influence the agent's tool arguments reads any JSON file the server process can reach, and on a write-enabled deployment overwrites one.\nL98: \nL99: sources\nL100: \nL101: 【14†AWS Security Bulletin 2026-164: Issue with AWS Serverless MCP Server (CVE-2026-116635)†aws.amazon.com】【15†GHSA-r6mm-377j-qg8m: Improper limitation of a pathname to a restricted directory in the AWS Serverless MCP Server†github.com】\nL102: \nL103: tags [Button: filesystem][Button: aws][Button: authorization][Button: advisory]\nL104: \nL105: 2026 · november · 02\nL106: [medium] CVE-2026-120492\nL107: ## Chainlit's SSRF Fix Added an Allowlist and a Semicolon Walks Past It 【16†#】\nL108: VulnCheck published CVE-2026-120492 on November 2, 2026 against Chainlit 2.12.0, credited to Gavin Blacklock and scored 6.3 under CVSS 4.0. `validate_mcp_url` does not reject the semicolon character, so a URL carrying `..;` segments submitted to the unauthenticated `/mcp` endpoint bypasses path-scoped entries in the operator's `allowed_urls`, on any origin that strips semicolon path parameters.\nL109: That allowlist is itself the remediation Chainlit shipped in 2.12.0 for GHSA-hvfh-5mj3-5f3j, the August 25, 2026 advisory covering SSRF through the same endpoint's `sse` and `streamable-http` transports, which made user-supplied MCP servers opt-in and required a default-deny `allowed_urls`.\nL110: commentary\nL111: \nL112: The August advisory was careful enough to publish its own residual risks: anonymous access on unauthenticated deployments, hostname matching with no private-IP blocking, header filtering by denylist. The one it did not list is that path scoping is only as good as the URL parser under it.\nL113: \nL114: impact\nL115: \nL116: An unauthenticated caller reaches paths the operator never granted, on any origin that treats `..;` as a path parameter rather than as part of the path.\nL117: \nL118: sources\nL119: 【17†VulnCheck: Chainlit 2.12.0 SSRF via semicolon path segment in MCP allowed_urls (CVE-2026-120492)†www.vulncheck.com】【18†GHSA-hvfh-5mj3-5f3j: SSRF via MCP SSE and streamable-http transports, the August advisory whose fix introduced allowed_urls†github.com】\nL120: \nL121: tags [Button: ssrf][Button: authorization][Button: advisory]\nL122: \nL123: 2026 · november · 01\nL124: \nL125: [high] CVE-2026-120338\nL126: ## FastMCP's Generative UI Tool Feeds Code Into a Sandbox That Gave Deno Everything 【19†#】\nL127: JFrog Security Research published JFSA-2026-001699278 on November 1, 2026, covering CVE-2026-120338 in `prefab-ui` 0.14.0 through 0.20.2 and credited to Yuval Moravchick. The Generative UI feature runs caller-supplied Python in Pyodide inside a Deno subprocess and is documented as isolation for untrusted code, including model-written UI.\nL128: `PyodideSandbox._start` launches Deno with unrestricted `--allow-read`, `--allow-write` and `--allow-net`, and the runner leaves the Deno API on the JavaScript global object, which Pyodide's `js` module proxies into Python. The usual remote path is FastMCP's `GenerativeUI` provider, which registers the MCP tool `generate_prefab_ui` and forwards that tool's `code` argument into the sandbox with no further checks.\nL129: JFrog scores it 10.0 against a FastMCP server with no authentication configured and 9.9 against one with authentication, giving the 9.9 as CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H and no version for the 10.0. The CVE record carries JFrog's 10.0 under CVSS 3.1. Version 0.21.0 revokes Deno's read, write and network permissions after startup and before any code is accepted.\nL130: 【20†Image: two monkeys looking around: everyone assumed somebody else sandboxed it】 EX.A two monkeys looking around: everyone assumed somebody else sandboxed it\nL131: \nL132: commentary\nL133: \nL134: The sandbox is the whole product here. Its own runner comment and the public docs describe the process as read-only, and `--allow-write` had been in the launcher since 0.14.0. Filed High rather than Critical because Generative UI is opt-in and the host has to have Deno installed, neither of which a default deployment does.\nL135: \nL136: impact\n"
